{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014CCc6VqTJsQ4tPs9wK42Z1","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now I have enough information. Let me compile my findings:\n\n## Architectural Friction Report: Almanac Loop Engine\n\nBased on my analysis of the codebase (reading CONTEXT.md, examining ~5500 lines of lib/ code, reviewing tests and cmd/), here are the most substantial architectural friction points, ranked by leverage:\n\n### 1. **Converge overseer parser robustness vs. maintainability** (lib/converge-core.sh:231-385)\n**Problem**: The overseer output parser (`almanac_converge_overseer_parse`) is a 150+ line state machine that normalizes LLM output decorations (markdown bold, hashes, bullets) before parsing. While pragmatic (fixes real overseer parsing failures), it creates tight coupling between the overseer's prompt format contract and the parser's brittle string normalization. Adding a new format variant or fixing parse bugs requires editing deep state-machine logic inside a 1143-line file.\n\n**Why it matters**: This is hard-to-test — state machines need exhaustive case coverage, and the markdown-stripping regex patterns (line 279-286) are fragile. A fourth decorative style breaks silently. The parse-failure fallback (all defaults, \"CONTINUE\") masks real issues; adding instrumentation required line 340 `ALMANAC_CONVERGE_PARSE_NOTE` as an indirect output channel — a non-obvious seam for testing.\n\n**Files**: lib/converge-core.sh:231-385, lib/converge-core.sh:143-161 (schema validation is lightweight and testable, but the parser itself is not)\n\n---\n\n### 2. **Harden-core's 1977-line module hides two orthogonal concerns**\n**Problem**: lib/harden-core.sh bundles three distinct subsystems: (1) rubric lifecycle + approval gates (lines 69-303), (2) reviewer orchestration + finding ledger management (lines 329-771), and (3) ratification engine + conductor orchestration (lines 773-920). Each is ~200-400 lines and uses different interfaces. The file fails the deletion test — you cannot remove any part without losing the ability to handle findings, rubrics, or ratification in isolation.\n\n**Why it matters**: Reviewers, conductors, and ledger writers are called from different loop phases but live in the same module. Testing a single concern (e.g., \"does a reopened finding correctly update the ledger?\") requires sourcing the entire 1977-line file and all its TSV machinery. Adding a new ledger field (e.g., reviewer-provided suggestions) touches three separate awk blocks (lines 413-435, 576-591, 665-698) that are not called functions — they are inlined string patterns.\n\n**Files**: lib/harden-core.sh (entire module, especially lines 69-771)\n\n---\n\n### 3. **TSV schema fragility across run.sh + worker.sh + harden-core + converge-core**\n**Problem**: The canonical schemas live in two places: `almanac_loop_record_fields` (run.sh:41-46, 10 fields) and `almanac_loop_worker_record_fields` (worker.sh:29-34, 9 fields). Readers are scattered: `almanac_harden_findings_tsv` (harden-core:385) parses reviewer JSON into TSV via awk, `almanac_harden_ledger_append_entry` writes 10 positional args (harden-core:521), and `almanac_converge_overseer_parse` uses globals instead of returning a TSV row. When adding a field to the run record (e.g., a new \"goal_context\" for converge), writers must know to call `record_set` but readers in harden-core still use manual awk field parsing (line 414-422, a fallback when jq is absent).\n\n**Why it matters**: The \"field list lives in one place\" contract is enforced only for run/worker records via `almanac_loop_tsv_record_set` (run.sh:75-108, a deep module). But harden's findings ledger (harden-core:521-574) encodes field order as positional printf args — reordering requires updating 6 callsites. The converge overseer parser (converge-core:231) returns data via globals (`ALMANAC_CONVERGE_VERDICT`, etc.), violating the TSV row contract entirely. A future \"findings export for analysis\" feature would need to invent yet another schema reader.\n\n**Files**: lib/run.sh:41-46, 75-108; lib/worker.sh:29-34, 41; lib/harden-core.sh:385-435 (awk parser), 521-574 (ledger writer); lib/converge-core.sh:231-355 (globals-based output)\n\n---\n\n### 4. **Harden's conductor provider is resolved per-role but not memoized across ratifications**\n**Problem**: Each of the ~5 open blocking findings triggers a call to `almanac_harden_demo_reproduces` (harden-core:845), which calls `almanac_harden_role_field` (harden-core:62) twice per finding to resolve the conductor provider and model. The role resolver is loop-agnostic and expensive-looking (role.sh:39-75 does env-key assembly and indirect expansion), but it is called O(findings) times in a loop phase that could batch. The provider/model are static for the run but inferred on demand from environment at line 866.\n\n**Why it matters**: Not a performance blocker today (findings are rare), but the pattern is untestable in isolation — to test \"does a blocking finding correctly invoke the conductor?\", you must set up role env (e.g., `HARDEN_CONDUCTOR_PROVIDER=claude`), call the orchestrator, then observe provider calls. The seam `almanac_harden_demo_reproduces` is the execution boundary, but it does not own provider resolution — that lives hidden in the role module. A future optimization (e.g., \"run conductor in parallel across findings\") would need to extract and cache the provider tuple outside the loop.\n\n**Files**: lib/harden-core.sh:845-881 (seam), lines 62-67 (role resolution), lib/role.sh:39-75 (expensive-looking)\n\n---\n\n### 5. **Prompt generation is mixed with orchestration in converge-core**\n**Problem**: `almanac_converge_worker_prompt` (converge-core:85-142), `almanac_converge_overseer_prompt` (converge-core:167-229), and `almanac_converge_ensure_prompt_template` (converge-core:63-83) are pure template generators but are called from orchestration code that also creates temp files, runs agents, and manages state. The prompt's structure (sections delimited by `===== NAME =====`) is embedded in two places: the prompt writer (lines 100-142) and the parser that reads it back (converge-core:143-161). If a future \"steer\" feature adds a new `STEER` section, you update the prompt, the parser, AND the orchestration code that calls it (line 131-141).\n\n**Why it matters**: The prompts encode schemas (the four-field VERDICT/REASON/STEER/GOAL_UPDATE contract, line 215) that the parser must know. Updating either breaks the contract. Neither the prompt nor the parser is testable standalone — they are entangled. The rubric-immutability guard (harden-core:258-302) is a better pattern: snapshot → mutate → verify; converge offers no equivalent guard, so a prompt edit mid-loop could corrupt the parsing state.\n\n**Files**: lib/converge-core.sh:63-142 (prompt writers), 143-161 (parser), 85-142 (orchestration)\n\n---\n\n### 6. **Shallow module: feedback.sh owns detection but not configuration**\n**Problem**: lib/feedback.sh (121 lines) detects feedback loops from marker files (package.json → npm test, Cargo.toml → cargo test) and runs them. The detection is hard-coded in `_almanac_loop_feedback_rows` (lines 19-55) — each marker block is one printf command + one or two command rows. Adding support for a new language (e.g., `ruff.toml` → ruff) requires adding a block, but the interface is \"here is a marker file, here are its feedback commands\" — very shallow. The runner (lines 90-121) is the deep part (exec isolation, verdict logging), but 40% of the module is the shallow marker-to-command table.\n\n**Why it matters**: Tests validate the detection (tests/test-feedback.sh:189) by asserting exact command strings, not by using a data-driven table. Adding a marker requires a code change + a test update, when a YAML config would separate concerns. Ralph consumes these commands (via the shared \"Feedback detection + runner\" contract), so changes ripple. The module is not pulling its weight as a reusable library — it is more a configuration point that happens to be code.\n\n**Files**: lib/feedback.sh:19-55 (shallow), 90-121 (deep)\n\n---\n\n### 7. **Converge/Harden loops both compose a convergence pattern but diverged implementations**\n**Problem**: Converge (converge-core.sh:1143 lines) and Harden (harden-core.sh:1977 lines) both follow an outer loop → inner agent → adjudication pattern. Converge uses an overseer (returns VERDICT/REASON/STEER/GOAL_UPDATE), Harden uses a ratifier (returns REPRODUCES/NOT). The abstract loop structure is nearly identical:\n- Seed state (rubric vs. goal)\n- N rounds: spawn workers, collect results, adjudicate, decide continue/stop/steer\n- Finalize (convergence.md vs. harden end-state)\n\nBut there is no shared convergence framework. If a third loop (e.g., \"iterate a test suite to convergence\") were added, it would duplicate both. The decision logic (stop/steer/continue) is inlined in each, not a pluggable predicate.\n\n**Why it matters**: Bug fixes (e.g., the overseer parser lenience added in line 251-264) do not generalize to Harden. The loop-adapter seam (lib/loops.sh) covers launch/control but not the convergence semantics — that would require a second seam for the convergence predicate. Without it, a fourth loop costs ~2000 lines of copy-pasted state machine + decision logic.\n\n**Files**: lib/harden-core.sh (entire module), lib/converge-core.sh (entire module), lib/loops.sh (seams are too shallow to cover convergence semantics)\n\n---\n\n### Summary\n\n**Highest-leverage improvements** (in order):\n1. **Extract harden's ratification engine (lines 773-920) into a separate module** — it is testable in isolation (given a demonstration, a provider tuple, return reproduces/not); decouples from the ledger/rubric/reviewer orchestration.\n2. **Unify TSV output across harden-core, converge-core, and run.sh** — converge overseer should return `VERDICT\\t...\\tGOAL_UPDATE` (TSV row), not globals; harden ledger should use `record_set` not positional printf; centralizes schema definition and makes readers pluggable.\n3. **Extract a convergence-loop framework** from harden + converge that owns the outer round loop, adjudication gates, and steer/stop logic — each concrete loop plugs in a decision predicate (ratify vs. overseer verdict) rather than reimplementing the loop.\n4. **Move prompt generation into a separate module** from converge orchestration — prompts + parsers live together, tested as a unit; orchestration calls a pure \"generate prompt\" function and a pure \"parse result\" function.\n5. **Harden's rubric/ledger/findings machinery (lines 69-771) should split** — rubric lifecycle is independent; findings ledger is independent; only the orchestration threads them together. Testing \"can I add a finding?\" should not require a full harden-core source.\n\nThe codebase is well-designed at the seams (loop-adapter, provider-adapter, role resolution) but large monolithic modules hide orthogonal concerns and make incremental testing hard."}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8f5db91c-a278-450c-98f6-32d4d1454f5d","uuid":"31d67a43-470c-4f30-ab84-835ea1253411","timestamp":"2026-05-27T07:59:47.293Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"Explore the almanac codebase at /Users/jakubneumann/Documents/code/neumie/almanac and report architectural friction. The codebase is a bash-based skill/loop engine for Claude Code & Codex. The domain model is documented in /Users/jakubneumann/Documents/code/neumie/almanac/CONTEXT.md (read it first).\n\nKey directories:\n- `lib/` — engine modules (loop adapters, providers, run registry, agent dispatch, etc.)\n- `lib/loops/` — loop adapters (ralph, harden, converge)\n- `lib/providers/` — provider adapters (claude, codex)\n- `cmd/` — CLI subcommands\n- `skills/` — skill definitions (nested by category)\n- `tests/` — test suite\n\nUse the Glossary terms exactly:\n- **Module** — anything with an interface and an implementation\n- **Interface** — types, invariants, error modes, ordering, config — everything a caller must know\n- **Depth** — leverage at the interface; deep = lots of behaviour behind small interface\n- **Shallow** — interface nearly as complex as the implementation\n- **Seam** — where an interface lives\n- **Adapter** — concrete satisfier of a seam\n- **Deletion test** — would removing the module concentrate complexity, or just move it?\n\nFor each piece of friction, report:\n- **Files** involved\n- **Problem** — why architecture causes friction (be specific: shallow module, leaky abstraction, hard-to-test surface, etc.)\n- **Why it matters** for testability and AI-navigability\n\nLook for:\n1. Shallow modules (interface ≈ implementation complexity) — but only ones substantial enough to matter\n2. Modules pulled out just for testability where the real bugs are how they're called\n3. Tightly-coupled modules leaking across their seams\n4. Untested code paths or hard-to-test interfaces\n5. Pass-through layers that fail the deletion test\n6. Per-loop or per-provider branches that should be adapter-dispatched (CONTEXT.md emphasizes auto-discovery)\n7. Status TSV / record schemas that drift between writers and readers\n8. Things that look like the same concern split across multiple files\n9. Long files (>500 lines) that may be hiding multiple deep modules\n\nFocus on **substantial** architectural improvements, not nitpicks. Skip cosmetic or trivial issues. The previous convergence runs (see docs/plans/converge/ logs) have reportedly exhausted obvious improvements — be discerning.\n\nRead the lib/*.sh files including loop-launcher.sh, run.sh, worker.sh, agent.sh, harden-core.sh, converge-core.sh, loops.sh, providers/*.sh, loops/*.sh. Skim the cmd/ scripts. Note line counts for the larger files.\n\nReport under 600 words with a ranked list (highest leverage first) of candidate improvements. Use specific file:line citations.","agentId":"aae694d8cda0d13e9","agentType":"Explore","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now I have enough information. Let me compile my findings:\n\n## Architectural Friction Report: Almanac Loop Engine\n\nBased on my analysis of the codebase (reading CONTEXT.md, examining ~5500 lines of lib/ code, reviewing tests and cmd/), here are the most substantial architectural friction points, ranked by leverage:\n\n### 1. **Converge overseer parser robustness vs. maintainability** (lib/converge-core.sh:231-385)\n**Problem**: The overseer output parser (`almanac_converge_overseer_parse`) is a 150+ line state machine that normalizes LLM output decorations (markdown bold, hashes, bullets) before parsing. While pragmatic (fixes real overseer parsing failures), it creates tight coupling between the overseer's prompt format contract and the parser's brittle string normalization. Adding a new format variant or fixing parse bugs requires editing deep state-machine logic inside a 1143-line file.\n\n**Why it matters**: This is hard-to-test — state machines need exhaustive case coverage, and the markdown-stripping regex patterns (line 279-286) are fragile. A fourth decorative style breaks silently. The parse-failure fallback (all defaults, \"CONTINUE\") masks real issues; adding instrumentation required line 340 `ALMANAC_CONVERGE_PARSE_NOTE` as an indirect output channel — a non-obvious seam for testing.\n\n**Files**: lib/converge-core.sh:231-385, lib/converge-core.sh:143-161 (schema validation is lightweight and testable, but the parser itself is not)\n\n---\n\n### 2. **Harden-core's 1977-line module hides two orthogonal concerns**\n**Problem**: lib/harden-core.sh bundles three distinct subsystems: (1) rubric lifecycle + approval gates (lines 69-303), (2) reviewer orchestration + finding ledger management (lines 329-771), and (3) ratification engine + conductor orchestration (lines 773-920). Each is ~200-400 lines and uses different interfaces. The file fails the deletion test — you cannot remove any part without losing the ability to handle findings, rubrics, or ratification in isolation.\n\n**Why it matters**: Reviewers, conductors, and ledger writers are called from different loop phases but live in the same module. Testing a single concern (e.g., \"does a reopened finding correctly update the ledger?\") requires sourcing the entire 1977-line file and all its TSV machinery. Adding a new ledger field (e.g., reviewer-provided suggestions) touches three separate awk blocks (lines 413-435, 576-591, 665-698) that are not called functions — they are inlined string patterns.\n\n**Files**: lib/harden-core.sh (entire module, especially lines 69-771)\n\n---\n\n### 3. **TSV schema fragility across run.sh + worker.sh + harden-core + converge-core**\n**Problem**: The canonical schemas live in two places: `almanac_loop_record_fields` (run.sh:41-46, 10 fields) and `almanac_loop_worker_record_fields` (worker.sh:29-34, 9 fields). Readers are scattered: `almanac_harden_findings_tsv` (harden-core:385) parses reviewer JSON into TSV via awk, `almanac_harden_ledger_append_entry` writes 10 positional args (harden-core:521), and `almanac_converge_overseer_parse` uses globals instead of returning a TSV row. When adding a field to the run record (e.g., a new \"goal_context\" for converge), writers must know to call `record_set` but readers in harden-core still use manual awk field parsing (line 414-422, a fallback when jq is absent).\n\n**Why it matters**: The \"field list lives in one place\" contract is enforced only for run/worker records via `almanac_loop_tsv_record_set` (run.sh:75-108, a deep module). But harden's findings ledger (harden-core:521-574) encodes field order as positional printf args — reordering requires updating 6 callsites. The converge overseer parser (converge-core:231) returns data via globals (`ALMANAC_CONVERGE_VERDICT`, etc.), violating the TSV row contract entirely. A future \"findings export for analysis\" feature would need to invent yet another schema reader.\n\n**Files**: lib/run.sh:41-46, 75-108; lib/worker.sh:29-34, 41; lib/harden-core.sh:385-435 (awk parser), 521-574 (ledger writer); lib/converge-core.sh:231-355 (globals-based output)\n\n---\n\n### 4. **Harden's conductor provider is resolved per-role but not memoized across ratifications**\n**Problem**: Each of the ~5 open blocking findings triggers a call to `almanac_harden_demo_reproduces` (harden-core:845), which calls `almanac_harden_role_field` (harden-core:62) twice per finding to resolve the conductor provider and model. The role resolver is loop-agnostic and expensive-looking (role.sh:39-75 does env-key assembly and indirect expansion), but it is called O(findings) times in a loop phase that could batch. The provider/model are static for the run but inferred on demand from environment at line 866.\n\n**Why it matters**: Not a performance blocker today (findings are rare), but the pattern is untestable in isolation — to test \"does a blocking finding correctly invoke the conductor?\", you must set up role env (e.g., `HARDEN_CONDUCTOR_PROVIDER=claude`), call the orchestrator, then observe provider calls. The seam `almanac_harden_demo_reproduces` is the execution boundary, but it does not own provider resolution — that lives hidden in the role module. A future optimization (e.g., \"run conductor in parallel across findings\") would need to extract and cache the provider tuple outside the loop.\n\n**Files**: lib/harden-core.sh:845-881 (seam), lines 62-67 (role resolution), lib/role.sh:39-75 (expensive-looking)\n\n---\n\n### 5. **Prompt generation is mixed with orchestration in converge-core**\n**Problem**: `almanac_converge_worker_prompt` (converge-core:85-142), `almanac_converge_overseer_prompt` (converge-core:167-229), and `almanac_converge_ensure_prompt_template` (converge-core:63-83) are pure template generators but are called from orchestration code that also creates temp files, runs agents, and manages state. The prompt's structure (sections delimited by `===== NAME =====`) is embedded in two places: the prompt writer (lines 100-142) and the parser that reads it back (converge-core:143-161). If a future \"steer\" feature adds a new `STEER` section, you update the prompt, the parser, AND the orchestration code that calls it (line 131-141).\n\n**Why it matters**: The prompts encode schemas (the four-field VERDICT/REASON/STEER/GOAL_UPDATE contract, line 215) that the parser must know. Updating either breaks the contract. Neither the prompt nor the parser is testable standalone — they are entangled. The rubric-immutability guard (harden-core:258-302) is a better pattern: snapshot → mutate → verify; converge offers no equivalent guard, so a prompt edit mid-loop could corrupt the parsing state.\n\n**Files**: lib/converge-core.sh:63-142 (prompt writers), 143-161 (parser), 85-142 (orchestration)\n\n---\n\n### 6. **Shallow module: feedback.sh owns detection but not configuration**\n**Problem**: lib/feedback.sh (121 lines) detects feedback loops from marker files (package.json → npm test, Cargo.toml → cargo test) and runs them. The detection is hard-coded in `_almanac_loop_feedback_rows` (lines 19-55) — each marker block is one printf command + one or two command rows. Adding support for a new language (e.g., `ruff.toml` → ruff) requires adding a block, but the interface is \"here is a marker file, here are its feedback commands\" — very shallow. The runner (lines 90-121) is the deep part (exec isolation, verdict logging), but 40% of the module is the shallow marker-to-command table.\n\n**Why it matters**: Tests validate the detection (tests/test-feedback.sh:189) by asserting exact command strings, not by using a data-driven table. Adding a marker requires a code change + a test update, when a YAML config would separate concerns. Ralph consumes these commands (via the shared \"Feedback detection + runner\" contract), so changes ripple. The module is not pulling its weight as a reusable library — it is more a configuration point that happens to be code.\n\n**Files**: lib/feedback.sh:19-55 (shallow), 90-121 (deep)\n\n---\n\n### 7. **Converge/Harden loops both compose a convergence pattern but diverged implementations**\n**Problem**: Converge (converge-core.sh:1143 lines) and Harden (harden-core.sh:1977 lines) both follow an outer loop → inner agent → adjudication pattern. Converge uses an overseer (returns VERDICT/REASON/STEER/GOAL_UPDATE), Harden uses a ratifier (returns REPRODUCES/NOT). The abstract loop structure is nearly identical:\n- Seed state (rubric vs. goal)\n- N rounds: spawn workers, collect results, adjudicate, decide continue/stop/steer\n- Finalize (convergence.md vs. harden end-state)\n\nBut there is no shared convergence framework. If a third loop (e.g., \"iterate a test suite to convergence\") were added, it would duplicate both. The decision logic (stop/steer/continue) is inlined in each, not a pluggable predicate.\n\n**Why it matters**: Bug fixes (e.g., the overseer parser lenience added in line 251-264) do not generalize to Harden. The loop-adapter seam (lib/loops.sh) covers launch/control but not the convergence semantics — that would require a second seam for the convergence predicate. Without it, a fourth loop costs ~2000 lines of copy-pasted state machine + decision logic.\n\n**Files**: lib/harden-core.sh (entire module), lib/converge-core.sh (entire module), lib/loops.sh (seams are too shallow to cover convergence semantics)\n\n---\n\n### Summary\n\n**Highest-leverage improvements** (in order):\n1. **Extract harden's ratification engine (lines 773-920) into a separate module** — it is testable in isolation (given a demonstration, a provider tuple, return reproduces/not); decouples from the ledger/rubric/reviewer orchestration.\n2. **Unify TSV output across harden-core, converge-core, and run.sh** — converge overseer should return `VERDICT\\t...\\tGOAL_UPDATE` (TSV row), not globals; harden ledger should use `record_set` not positional printf; centralizes schema definition and makes readers pluggable.\n3. **Extract a convergence-loop framework** from harden + converge that owns the outer round loop, adjudication gates, and steer/stop logic — each concrete loop plugs in a decision predicate (ratify vs. overseer verdict) rather than reimplementing the loop.\n4. **Move prompt generation into a separate module** from converge orchestration — prompts + parsers live together, tested as a unit; orchestration calls a pure \"generate prompt\" function and a pure \"parse result\" function.\n5. **Harden's rubric/ledger/findings machinery (lines 69-771) should split** — rubric lifecycle is independent; findings ledger is independent; only the orchestration threads them together. Testing \"can I add a finding?\" should not require a full harden-core source.\n\nThe codebase is well-designed at the seams (loop-adapter, provider-adapter, role resolution) but large monolithic modules hide orthogonal concerns and make incremental testing hard."}],"totalDurationMs":70527,"totalTokens":102377,"totalToolUseCount":29,"usage":{"input_tokens":7,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4627,"cache_read_input_tokens":94814,"output_tokens":2929,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4627},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":7,"output_tokens":2929,"cache_read_input_tokens":94814,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4627,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4627,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":20,"searchCount":1,"bashCount":8,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":0}}}
